<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Dawaro</h1><p class="st-meta">1280 CE – 1580 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim sultanate of south-eastern Ethiopia that rebelled repeatedly against the Christian emperors before being swept away by the Oromo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawaro">Dawaro</a> lay south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awash-river">Awash</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shebelle-river">Wabi Shebelle</a> river, and appears in sources from the 13th century as one of the Muslim states of the Ethiopian interior. Around 1332 its ruler, Haydara, joined a revolt against the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> and was defeated, making the state tributary to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawaro">Dawaro</a> was ruled by local Muslim princes under imperial overlordship and grew rich from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, horses and position on trade routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>. The emperors installed governors when it rebelled. It lay on the frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal</a>, so its loyalties shifted with the balance of power.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1530s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawaro">Dawaro</a> joined the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</a> in his conquest of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. After his death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo-migrations">Oromo migrations</a> overran the territory in the second half of the 16th century, and Dawaro ceased to exist as a state.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1280 CE – 1580 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Horn and East Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.17%;width:5.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1280 CE – 1580 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Haydara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dawaro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1430&amp;focus=sultanate-of-dawaro" aria-label="Sultanate of Dawaro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5843 1830 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Dawaro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6163 2086L6029 1964M6163 2086L6135 2044"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0M6135 2044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6163 2086h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6163 2086h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1430&amp;focus=sultanate-of-dawaro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

